WEBVTT

00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:07.506
<b>Emma:</b>	“Welcome to Denver Airport. How can I help you today?”

00:00:08.646 --> 00:00:14.965
I handed my passport and flight confirmation to the woman at the United Airlines check-in.

00:00:15.823 --> 00:00:20.889
I was on my way to Costa Rica at the beginning of the trip of a lifetime,

00:00:21.279 --> 00:00:23.939
traveling around Central America.

00:00:24.936 --> 00:00:29.277
The woman checked in my backpack and handed me my boarding pass.

00:00:30.162 --> 00:00:37.568
“Lucky you, going to California,” she smiled. “Your gate is 37C.”

00:00:38.428 --> 00:00:41.838
“No. I booked a ticket to San José.”

00:00:42.619 --> 00:00:47.748
“Yes, Ms. Benns. Your ticket is for San José, in California.”

00:00:48.589 --> 00:00:57.410
I was horrified! “I’m so sorry, but I need your help. My ticket is for the wrong San José.”

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:00.966
This time she didn’t smile.

00:01:01.715 --> 00:01:04.938
She pointed to the help desk for United Airlines.

00:01:05.463 --> 00:01:08.083
The next few hours were terrible.

00:01:08.831 --> 00:01:11.599
The help desk assistant wasn’t helpful.

00:01:12.608 --> 00:01:16.273
“I’m sorry. We can’t change your ticket.”

00:01:17.156 --> 00:01:21.616
I tried to call my travel agent, hoping they could solve the problem.

00:01:22.223 --> 00:01:23.048
No luck.

00:01:23.992 --> 00:01:27.198
A message arrived on my phone from my dad.

00:01:28.176 --> 00:01:34.065
It was a link to the Dionne Warwick song "Do you know the way to San Jose?"

00:01:34.554 --> 00:01:35.708
Not funny, Dad.

00:01:36.737 --> 00:01:42.098
Then a new attendant arrived. "What’s the problem?" she asked.

00:01:42.730 --> 00:01:44.715
I explained the situation.

00:01:45.732 --> 00:01:51.194
“Of course we can book a new flight for you, Ms. Benns.” I hugged her.

00:01:52.081 --> 00:01:57.280
She immediately booked me onto a cheap flight to San José, Costa Rica.

00:01:58.192 --> 00:02:01.033
Suddenly, I remembered my backpack.

00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:03.314
But it was too late.

00:02:04.426 --> 00:02:07.238
“Did you check in my luggage?” I asked.

00:02:07.984 --> 00:02:12.094
“No. Unfortunately, it’s on the way to California.”

00:02:13.292 --> 00:02:20.284
So, after 72 hours, four flights, and three days without luggage,

00:02:20.830 --> 00:02:26.961
I can say that I know the long way to San José.

00:02:27.918 --> 00:02:31.021
And I have one important lesson for you:

00:02:31.682 --> 00:02:35.803
always take spare underwear in your carry-on luggage.

00:02:36.422 --> 00:02:43.290
And if you can fit extra jeans, a T-shirt, and a sweater in there, too, DO THAT!